    id: "troubleshooting",
    title: "Troubleshooting & Common Fixes",
    summary: "Exhaustive field guide and diagnostic playbook for resolving port conflicts, Docker socket permissions, CDP browser stalls, OOM limits, and queue recovery.",
    category: "CONFIGURATION / TROUBLESHOOTING",
    content: "# Troubleshooting & Field Diagnostics Playbook\n\nThis guide covers real-world failure modes, edge cases, diagnostic terminal commands, and verified fixes across the entire StackPilot platform.\n\n---\n\n## Diagnostic Triage Cheat Sheet\n\n```bash\n# 1. Check overall platform status and unhealthy containers\ndocker compose ps -a\n\n# 2. View real-time logs from backend and AI service\ndocker compose logs -f --tail=100 backend ai-service\n\n# 3. Verify PostgreSQL connectivity and database locks\ndocker exec -it stackpilot-postgres pg_isready -U stackpilot_admin -d stackpilot_platform\n\n# 4. Inspect Redis queue depth and active worker locks\ndocker exec -it stackpilot-redis redis-cli LLEN stackpilot:jobs:deployment\n\n# 5. Check host memory pressure and swap utilization\nfree -h && vmstat -s\n```\n\n---\n\n## 1. Port Binding Conflicts (`bind: address already in use`)\n\n### Symptom:\n`docker compose up` fails with:\n`Error response from daemon: driver failed programming external connectivity on endpoint stackpilot-postgres: Bind for 127.0.0.1:5433 failed: port is already allocated`\n\n### Root Cause:\nA local service (e.g. host PostgreSQL on 5432, local Redis on 6379, or Next.js dev server on 3000) is already listening on the requested port.\n\n### Resolution:\n1. Identify the process holding the port:\n   ```bash\n   # On Linux:\n   sudo ss -tulpn | grep -E ':(3000|8090|5433|6379|8010)'\n   \n   # On Windows (PowerShell):\n   Get-NetTCPConnection -LocalPort 5433, 8090, 3000 | Select-Object LocalPort, OwningProcess\n   ```\n2. Modify the host port mapping in your `.env` file instead of altering core configurations:\n   ```env\n   # Change conflicting port mappings\n   STACKPILOT_AI_SERVICE_PORT=8015\n   PROMETHEUS_PORT=9095\n   GRAFANA_PORT=3005\n   ```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Docker Socket Permission Denied (`group_add: [\"0\"]`)\n\n### Symptom:\nThe backend logs:\n`dial unix /var/run/docker.sock: connect: permission denied` or build jobs fail immediately with `Docker daemon unavailable`.\n\n### Root Cause:\nThe backend container runs as dedicated non-root user `uid 10001:gid 10001`. On Docker Desktop (Windows/macOS) and certain Linux installations, `/var/run/docker.sock` is owned by `root:root` with mode `0660`. Without supplementary group permissions, unprivileged users cannot write to the socket.\n\n### Resolution:\nVerify that `docker-compose.yml` includes the supplementary group entry:\n```yaml\nbackend:\n  user: \"10001:10001\"\n  group_add:\n    - \"0\" # Grants socket access while preserving non-root UID 10001\n```\nOn Linux hosts with an existing `docker` group, find the GID (`getent group docker | cut -d: -f3`) and add that GID to `group_add`.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Uploads Workspace Permission Failures (`uploads-init`)\n\n### Symptom:\nDeployments fail with:\n`Unable to prepare generated application build workspace: Permission denied` or `EACCES: permission denied, mkdir '/app/uploads/builds'`.\n\n### Root Cause:\nThe host directory `./uploads` was historically initialized by root, preventing container user `10001` from writing build artifacts.\n\n### Resolution:\nEnsure the `uploads-init` service executes before the backend:\n```bash\ndocker compose up uploads-init\n```\nOr manually reset permissions on the host:\n```bash\nsudo chown -R 10001:10001 ./uploads\nsudo chmod -R u+rwX,g+rwX ./uploads\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. Real-Time Screencast Disconnects & CDP Timeouts\n\n### Symptom:\nThe visual browser canvas displays \"Connecting...\" indefinitely, or logs report:\n`websockets.exceptions.ConnectionClosedError: code = 1006 (connection closed abnormally)`.\n\n### Diagnostic Commands:\n```bash\n# Verify Chromium debugging port is reachable\ncurl -fsS http://localhost:9222/json/version\n\n# Verify Xvfb virtual display socket exists\ndocker exec -it stackpilot-browser-sandbox ls -la /tmp/.X11-unix/\n\n# Check video streamer process\ndocker exec -it stackpilot-browser-sandbox ps aux | grep streamer.py\n```\n\n### Root Cause & Fix:\n1. **Stale Lockfiles**: If the sandbox container was force-killed, X11 lockfile `/tmp/.X11-unix/X99` or Chromium singleton lock `/tmp/chromium-data/SingletonLock` persists.\n2. Restart the sandbox container\u2014StackPilot's `entrypoint.sh` will purge stale locks automatically:\n   ```bash\n   docker compose restart browser-sandbox\n   ```\n3. **Internal URL Translation**: If testing `http://localhost:3000` inside the sandbox, ensure `to_container_accessible_url` translates this to `frontend:3000`. Inside Docker, `localhost` refers to the container itself, not the host!\n\n---\n\n## 5. Out-of-Memory (OOM) Killer on Low-Spec VPS (1GB / 2GB)\n\n### Symptom:\nContainers randomly exit with code `137`. System `dmesg` shows:\n`Out of memory: Killed process 12345 (postgres)` or `docker-compose build` aborts.\n\n### Resolution:\n1. **Enable a Swap File**: Linux cloud VPS instances frequently ship with zero swap enabled by default:\n   ```bash\n   sudo fallocate -l 2G /swapfile\n   sudo chmod 600 /swapfile\n   sudo mkswap /swapfile\n   sudo swapon /swapfile\n   echo '/swapfile none swap sw 0 0' | sudo tee -a /etc/fstab\n   ```\n2. **Switch to Lite Profile**:\n   If running on sub-2GB RAM without swap, do not boot the `monitoring` or `browser` profiles. Use the optimized Lite profile:\n   ```bash\n   docker compose -f docker-compose.lite.yml up -d\n   ```\n3. **Limit PostgreSQL Shared Buffers**:\n   Ensure `docker-compose.yml` retains the low-memory PostgreSQL tune:\n   `-c shared_buffers=32MB -c work_mem=4MB -c max_connections=25`\n\n---\n\n## 6. AI Service Rate Limits & Model Discovery Cache\n\n### Symptom:\nAI chat returns:\n`NVIDIA NIM API error 429: Too Many Requests` or slow response latency (> 30s).\n\n### Root Cause:\nRepeated cold requests trigger un-cached model probe sweeps across hundreds of NVIDIA NIM endpoints.\n\n### Resolution:\n1. StackPilot serializes model probe sweeps using `MODEL_PROBE_LOCK` and caches probe results for 3600 seconds (`MODEL_PROBE_CACHE_TTL`).\n2. Pin your model explicitly in `.env` to bypass dynamic probing:\n   ```env\n   STACKPILOT_AI_MODEL=meta/llama-3.1-70b-instruct\n   NVIDIA_NIM_DISCOVER_MODELS=false\n   ```\n3. For local or offline development, switch provider to a local Ollama instance:\n   ```env\n   STACKPILOT_AI_PROVIDER=openai_compatible\n   OPENAI_COMPATIBLE_BASE_URL=http://host.docker.internal:11434/v1\n   OPENAI_COMPATIBLE_MODEL=llama3.2\n   ```\n\n---\n\n## 7. Redis Deployment Queue Worker Stall\n\n### Symptom:\nDeployments remain in `queued` state forever; build logs never start.\n\n### Diagnostic & Fix:\n1. Verify worker threads are active in backend logs:\n   ```bash\n   docker logs stackpilot-backend | grep -i \"worker\"\n   ```\n2. Check Redis queue length:\n   ```bash\n   docker exec -it stackpilot-redis redis-cli LLEN stackpilot:jobs:deployment\n   ```\n3. If the queue is non-empty but workers are idle, restart the backend service. StackPilot's `recoverInterruptedJobs()` routine will detect the orphaned jobs, release locks, and re-enqueue them automatically:\n   ```bash\n   docker compose restart backend\n   ```\n",
